title: Hints
tags: GUI
kind: guide
aliases: hints link-hints avy help-hints
summary: g h puts a label of letters on everything clickable in view of the focused panel, in any panel type; typing a label activates it, as a click would.

<<key "hints:open" "g h">> puts a label of one or more letters on everything that can
be clicked in the focused panel, the way avy jumps in Emacs. Typing a label activates
its element; while a label is half typed, only the ones it starts are shown, and a
letter no label continues is ignored. Escape cancels and leaves the panel as it was.

It works in every panel type because the shell does it: a panel has nothing to
provide, as for [[Find in a panel]].

!! What is labelled

* the controls: links, buttons, text zones, checkboxes, radio buttons, selects, editable text, and what has one of those roles;
* whatever the panel's style gives a pointer cursor (`cursor: pointer`): this is how a row of a list, or a tab, says that it can be clicked. What lies inside such an element shares its pointer and gets no label of its own, unless it is a control;
* a `<label>` and its control are one: only the control gets a label.

A panel whose element reacts to a click without any of these has to give it
`cursor: pointer`, which it owes the mouse anyway.

Only what is in view is labelled, so the labels stay short: one letter for up to 26
elements, two beyond. Neither what is disabled or hidden is, nor what a scrolling part
of the panel cuts off. What is covered by something else of the same panel is still
labelled. To reach an element further down, scroll to it first.

!! What activating does

A text zone, a number or date field, a select or editable text gets the focus, ready
to type in. Everything else gets a click: a button runs, a checkbox toggles, a row is
selected, as with the mouse ([[Opening a row]]).

!! While the labels show

The letters go to a small box of their own at the bottom right (`focus = "hints"`), so
no other key takes them. The labels mark where things were: scrolling, resizing the
window, changing panel or leaving the box ends them. If the panel redraws under them,
a label typed for an element that is gone says so in the status bar and clicks nothing.

Like a click, activating an element is not recorded in a macro ([[Macros]]).
